      "writtenOn": "2024-07-22T22:10:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not a fan of exposing the ringbuffer fd publicly, because it is ripe for various kinds of abuse leading to fd leakage - and those sorts of problems are then an absolute nightmare to track down.\n\nmRingFd is \u0027protected\u0027 so AFAICT you *could* always create a new subclass to expose it (either \u0027mRingFd\u0027 directly or via a GetIOFd() function) if you really want/need to.\n\nI\u0027d possibly be willing to merge a wrapper (or wrappers?) around:\n  https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/epoll_ctl.2.html EPOLL_CTL_{ADD,MOD,DEL}\nthat would take the epoll fd on input and add/mod/del the ring buffer fd to it.\n\nBut I\u0027m not 100% sure (I\u0027ve never really used epoll myself) if that\u0027s actually\nsufficient for your use case, and indeed it might still leak the fd (though perhaps in a more \u0027hidden\u0027 and thus acceptable fashion).",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 146,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 146,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "b9f4f044bae7793f25d57239a5da43f7d6ffc3af",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fb0a2b84_fc246a3b",
        "filename": "staticlibs/native/bpf_headers/include/bpf/BpfRingbuf.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 146,
      "author": {
        "id": 2068221
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-23T06:49:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I understand your concern regarding the fd leakage. And as you say, we can always solve it with inheritance, so this patch is not crucial. The BpfRingbuf\u0027s private constructor make it so that sub-classes adding the GetIOFd() (or similar) needs to inherit from BpfRingBuf() and copy Create() and ConsumeAll() from BpfRing(), which is doable but a bit annoying.\n\nHaving the described epoll wrappers would solve our use-case. I don\u0027t think there\u0027s a risk of fd leakage with an epoll wrapper approach. As far as I know, the only way epoll can leak the fd\u0027s is if you fill the data field of the struct epoll_event passed to epoll_ctl(EPOLL_CTL_ADD, ...) with the fd, since that data field will be returned back once epoll_wait() returns. But if we encapsulate the epoll_ctl() in wrappers that will not be possible.",
      "parentUuid": "4e0ba557_c5857090",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 146,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 146,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "b9f4f044bae7793f25d57239a5da43f7d6ffc3af",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "067ee3ef_b282c451",
        "filename": "staticlibs/native/bpf_headers/include/bpf/BpfRingbuf.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 146,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003247
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-23T18:26:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "(a) A patch to make the private constructor (in line 153 I understand) protected would be fine.\n\n(b) Yeah, I think if we wrap epoll_ctl() then indeed epoll_wait() on the externally visible epoll fd will quite likely return the event causing fd.  That seems to be unavoidable...  Technically that would require wrapping epoll_wait() as well, but that seems infeasible [think of trying to simultaneously wait on 2 different bpf ringbuffers]... so I think epoll_wait() has to remain external to the class.  At best we can provide some sort of \u0027bool isThisYourEpollEvent(epoll_event)\u0027 helper... though not sure if that is even needed... since you could always just call ConsumeAll on any and all BpfRingBuffers any time you\u0027re woken up and can\u0027t recognize the fd.",
      "parentUuid": "fb0a2b84_fc246a3b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 146,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 146,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "b9f4f044bae7793f25d57239a5da43f7d6ffc3af",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3fc56444_4377020e",
        "filename": "staticlibs/native/bpf_headers/include/bpf/BpfRingbuf.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 146,
      "author": {
        "id": 2068221
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-24T06:50:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "a) Okay, I can submit a patch for that as well. Enabling easier sub-classing in in general would be nice. \n\nb) Hmm, I don\u0027t think that wrapping epoll_wait() is a good idea since we don\u0027t know what other things the user wants to wait on as well. But I think we can avoid the fd leakage without wrapping it.\n\nI see two way of design the epoll_ctl() wrapper and in particular how the epoll_data_t data field within the struct epoll_event is handled. Cause either we let the user pass the field to the wrapper method and let them decide what to fill the void*/int with, that also make them responsible for figuring out how to determine who owns the epoll events. This will not cause any leakage since the user don\u0027t know the fd.\n\nThe other option is to fill the field ourselves within the wrapper. And then we can either leave it blank, forcing the user to check all the BpfRingBuffers on wakeup which I don\u0027t think is that big of a problem. Or we fill it with some identifying data to allow some kind of \u0027bool isThisYourEpollEvent(epoll_event)\u0027 helper. Then passing the fd feels like that most natural approach, but I think putting the \"this pointer\" there could works as well eliminating leaks.\n\nI think I prefer the flexibility of the first option.",
      "parentUuid": "067ee3ef_b282c451",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 146,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 146,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "b9f4f044bae7793f25d57239a5da43f7d6ffc3af",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0f40f6d3_a56cb807",
        "filename": "staticlibs/native/bpf_headers/include/bpf/BpfRingbuf.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
